AI Avatar V1

Overview

AI Avatar V1 is a locally hosted, real time conversational system that combines speech, vision, and personality driven interaction into a single unified experience.

The system allows a user to speak naturally to an on screen avatar, which can listen, respond, and, when appropriate, interpret visual input from a live camera feed. The result is a multimodal conversational agent that feels significantly more interactive than a traditional chatbot.

This project represents Version 1 of the system, focused on establishing a stable, working foundation for real time audio, vision, and conversational flow.

⸻

What It Does
	•	Accepts live speech input via microphone
	•	Transcribes speech locally using whisper.cpp
	•	Maintains a running conversation with short term memory
	•	Responds using a local language model via vLLM
	•	Uses a live camera feed to interpret visual context when relevant
	•	Speaks responses back using a local TTS engine
	•	Displays an animated avatar with natural movement and lip sync

⸻

Avatar

The avatar is not a static image. It is designed to feel present, responsive, and alive.

It is built using layered visual frames that simulate natural human behaviour:
	•	Subtle body movement to avoid a rigid, static posture
	•	Randomised blinking to create a sense of awareness
	•	Lip synchronisation driven by audio playback
	•	Smooth transitions between idle, listening, and speaking states

The result is a character that stands naturally on screen and reacts in a way that feels continuous rather than mechanical.

Visually, the avatar presents as a space age digital character, but its behaviour is grounded in familiar human cues. When combined with real time speech and vision, this creates the impression of a conversational presence rather than a traditional interface.

⸻

Key Features

Real Time Conversation

The avatar listens continuously, detects when the user has finished speaking, and responds naturally without requiring button presses.

Vision Integration

When required, the system can process a camera image and use it to answer questions about:
	•	visible text
	•	objects
	•	posture or expression
	•	screen content

Vision is selectively used based on the user’s question, avoiding constant narration of the environment.

Short Term Memory

The system retains conversation history within the active session, allowing it to:
	•	recall previously seen information
	•	maintain context across turns
	•	reference earlier responses

Persona Modes

The system includes multiple behavioural modes:
	•	Intellectual: analytical and precise
	•	Light: relaxed and conversational
	•	Adult: confident, directive, and leading the interaction

Local First

All core processing is performed locally:
	•	no dependency on external APIs
	•	supports offline operation
	•	full control over data and behaviour

User Speech → Transcription → Language Model → Response → Speech Output
                                  ↑
                              Optional Vision Input

Components
	•	Speech to Text: whisper.cpp
	•	Language Model: vLLM serving a multimodal model (Qwen VL)
	•	Text to Speech: Piper
	•	Frontend: Browser based UI with JavaScript
	•	Backend: FastAPI server
	•	Vision Input: Live webcam frames encoded and passed to the model

⸻

How Vision Works
	•	The camera feed is captured periodically in the browser
	•	Frames are encoded and sent only when needed
	•	The model is prompted to use only relevant visual details
	•	Full scene descriptions are suppressed unless explicitly requested

This allows the system to behave more like a human observer:
	•	focusing on what matters
	•	ignoring irrelevant background information

⸻

Example Capabilities
	•	Reading text from a document held in front of the camera
	•	Recalling previously read information within the same conversation
	•	Describing relevant visual elements when asked
	•	Maintaining conversational flow while incorporating visual context

⸻

Limitations (V1)
	•	No persistent memory beyond the current session
	•	Vision is stateless between calls
	•	Persona is static and does not evolve over time
	•	No multi model orchestration or task routing
	•	Audio expression is limited by TTS capabilities

⸻

Purpose of V1

Version 1 establishes a working baseline for:
	•	real time multimodal interaction
	•	conversational flow with memory
	•	controlled use of vision
	•	local deployment

It is intentionally simple in architecture, with all logic handled through a single model loop.

⸻

Next Steps (Future Versions)

Future versions will introduce:
	•	a control layer for task routing
	•	separate models for vision, reasoning, and conversation
	•	structured memory and scene awareness
	•	improved emotional expression and speech delivery
	•	enhanced embodiment and tracking

⸻

Summary

AI Avatar V1 demonstrates that a locally hosted system can:
	•	see
	•	listen
	•	respond
	•	remember within context

and do so in a way that feels interactive, grounded, and visually alive.

This version serves as the foundation for building a more advanced, modular, and human like system in future iterations.
